%% The states one delivery moves through, and what moves it. A delivery is a single
%% (event, sink) pair; an event with three sinks has three of these running independently.
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> pending: event stored,<br/>one row per sink

    pending --> in_flight: worker claims it<br/>(next_attempt_at is due)

    in_flight --> delivered: 2xx
    in_flight --> pending: retryable failure —<br/>5xx, 408, 429, timeout,<br/>transport error
    in_flight --> exhausted: permanent failure —<br/>other 4xx, bad template,<br/>sink unavailable
    in_flight --> exhausted: attempts reached<br/>max_attempts

    pending --> pending: backoff window<br/>(base × 2ⁿ, capped, ± jitter)

    exhausted --> [*]: row written to dlq
    delivered --> [*]: swept after<br/>retention_days

    note right of pending
        A process killed while rows are in_flight
        leaves them with nothing running to finish
        them. requeue_incomplete() at startup returns
        every pending and in_flight row to pending and
        makes it due now — otherwise it is a lost event
        wearing a database row.
    end note

    note left of exhausted
        Permanent and retryable are separated on
        purpose. Five backed-off attempts at a 400
        only delay the moment the operator finds out,
        and treating a 503 as terminal throws away a
        delivery that would have succeeded in a minute.
    end note

%% Jitter is not decoration. After an outage every queued delivery becomes due at the same
%% instant; firing them in lockstep re-creates the load that caused the outage.
%%
%% An event is only swept once all of its deliveries have settled — sweeping one mid-retry would
%% delete the payload that retry is about to send.
